{
    "FormatVersion": 2,
    "DisplayName": "Altocumulus",
    "Notes": "The mid-level sheet of small lumps. Thin (700 m), high (3.2 km) and half as dense as a cumulus, so the sky reads through it - which is what distinguishes it from the stratocumulus it otherwise resembles. Placement scale 0.30 and anisotropy 1.50 - many small cells in rows, which is the 'mackerel sky'. It is the SAME pair of controls that make the lenticular next door a different kind of cloud rather than a thicker version of this one: this stretches ALONG the wind, that one across it.",
